(recognition of the fact that it was not the sun but the earth that moves) Tj
T* (sufficed to destroy the whole cosmography of the ancients. By disproving) Tj
T* (that law it might have been possible to retain the old conception of) Tj
T* (the movements of the bodies, but without disproving it, it would seem) Tj
T* (impossible to continue studying the Ptolemaic worlds. But even after) Tj
T* (the discovery of the law of Copernicus the Ptolemaic worlds were still) Tj
T* (studied for a long time.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (From the time the first person said and proved that the number of births) Tj
T* (or of crimes is subject to mathematical laws, and that this or that) Tj
T* (mode of government is determined by certain geographical and economic) Tj
T* (conditions, and that certain relations of population to soil produce) Tj
T* (migrations of peoples, the foundations on which history had been built) Tj
T* (were destroyed in their essence.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (By refuting these new laws the former view of history might have been) Tj
T* (retained; but without refuting them it would seem impossible to continue) Tj
T* (studying historic events as the results of man?s free will. For if a) Tj
T* (certain mode of government was established or certain migrations) Tj
T* (of peoples took place in consequence of such and such geographic,) Tj
T* (ethnographic, or economic conditions, then the free will of those) Tj
T* (individuals who appear to us to have established that mode of government) Tj
T* (or occasioned the migrations can no longer be regarded as the cause.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (And yet the former history continues to be studied side by side with the) Tj
T* (laws of statistics, geography, political economy, comparative philology,) Tj
T* (and geology, which directly contradict its assumptions.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The struggle between the old views and the new was long and stubbornly) Tj
T* (fought out in physical philosophy. Theology stood on guard for the) Tj
T* (old views and accused the new of violating revelation. But when truth) Tj
T* (conquered, theology established itself just as firmly on the new) Tj
T* (foundation.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Just as prolonged and stubborn is the struggle now proceeding between) Tj
T* (the old and the new conception of history, and theology in the same way) Tj
T* (stands on guard for the old view, and accuses the new view of subverting) Tj
T* (revelation.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (In the one case as in the other, on both sides the struggle provokes) Tj
T* (passion and stifles truth. On the one hand there is fear and regret for) Tj
T* (the loss of the whole edifice constructed through the ages, on the other) Tj
T* (is the passion for destruction.) Tj
T* () Tj
