(To the men who fought against the rising truths of physical philosophy,) Tj
T* (it seemed that if they admitted that truth it would destroy faith in) Tj
T* (God, in the creation of the firmament, and in the miracle of Joshua the) Tj
T* (son of Nun. To the defenders of the laws of Copernicus and Newton, to) Tj
T* (Voltaire for example, it seemed that the laws of astronomy destroyed) Tj
T* (religion, and he utilized the law of gravitation as a weapon against) Tj
T* (religion.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Just so it now seems as if we have only to admit the law of) Tj
T* (inevitability, to destroy the conception of the soul, of good and evil,) Tj
T* (and all the institutions of state and church that have been built up on) Tj
T* (those conceptions.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (So too, like Voltaire in his time, uninvited defenders of the law of) Tj
T* (inevitability today use that law as a weapon against religion, though) Tj
T* (the law of inevitability in history, like the law of Copernicus in) Tj
T* (astronomy, far from destroying, even strengthens the foundation on which) Tj
T* (the institutions of state and church are erected.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (As in the question of astronomy then, so in the question of history) Tj
T* (now, the whole difference of opinion is based on the recognition or) Tj
T* (nonrecognition of something absolute, serving as the measure of visible) Tj
T* (phenomena. In astronomy it was the immovability of the earth, in history) Tj
T* (it is the independence of personality?free will.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (As with astronomy the difficulty of recognizing the motion of the earth) Tj
T* (lay in abandoning the immediate sensation of the earth?s fixity and of) Tj
T* (the motion of the planets, so in history the difficulty of recognizing) Tj
T* (the subjection of personality to the laws of space, time, and cause) Tj
T* (lies in renouncing the direct feeling of the independence of one?s own) Tj
T* (personality. But as in astronomy the new view said: ?It is true that we) Tj
T* (do not feel the movement of the earth, but by admitting its immobility) Tj
T* (we arrive at absurdity, while by admitting its motion \(which we do not) Tj
T* (feel\) we arrive at laws,? so also in history the new view says: ?It is) Tj
T* (true that we are not conscious of our dependence, but by admitting our) Tj
T* (free will we arrive at absurdity, while by admitting our dependence on) Tj
T* (the external world, on time, and on cause, we arrive at laws.?) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (In the first case it was necessary to renounce the consciousness of an) Tj
T* (unreal immobility in space and to recognize a motion we did not feel;) Tj
T* (in the present case it is similarly necessary to renounce a freedom) Tj
T* (that does not exist, and to recognize a dependence of which we are not) Tj
T* (conscious.) Tj
